{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966810,"nonce":"DbFstCIpCLYUTagUof8CWNrb2J0=","title":"Closure / lambda bodies in zyntax bypass TypedCfgBuilder — control-flow statements silently","message":"zyntax's `translate_closure` (compiler/src/ssa.rs ~line 8458) runs `process_statement` / `translate_expression` directly on the lambda body instead of going through `TypedCfgBuilder::build_from_block`. That bypass had three cascading effects that combined to silently drop every control-flow statement inside any closure:\n\n1. `process_statement` had no `TypedStatement::Block` arm — grouped statement lists (Blinc's `match`-arm lowering emits these) hit `_ =\u003e {}` and never recursed.\n2. `process_statement::TypedStatement::If` required pre-built `block.successors` to be non-empty. Lambdas never populate them, so the if-statement degenerated into a no-op (both branches unreachable).\n3. `TypedExpression::Block` (which the parser uses to wrap lambda bodies) evaluated `Expression` / `Let` / `Return` statements but dropped everything else through `_ =\u003e {}`.\n\nThree more compose-bugs in the same neighborhood:\n\n- Nested `TypedExpression::If` clobbered itself: outer If wired `Branch(merge)` onto original `else_block_id`, severing any CondBranch a nested If had set there. Fix uses `continuation_block` to find each branch's actual tail.\n- New If branches initialised `definitions` to empty — `read_variable` then synthesised incomplete phis for every captured variable, which Cranelift lowered to block parameters and the matching `brif` ended up missing its arg list (`got 0, expected 1` verifier error). Fix inherits parent block definitions into then/else.\n- `translate_closure`'s tail unconditionally overwrote `entry_block_id`'s terminator with `Return`, clobbering any top-level if-statement's CondBranch. Fix walks blocks with `Unreachable` terminator and sets Return on each.\n- `TypedExpression::If` emitted a phi at merge whose ty was `result_type` (from `expr.ty`) but whose incoming values came from the branch bodies' last expressions. Statement-like ifs (`expr.ty = Unit` → Void) mismatched i32/i64 against Void. Fix skips the phi when result_type is Void.\n\n**Why:** Lambda bodies are first-class in Blinc — every `on_click`, every `subscribe`, every `effect` is a closure that may contain match / if / while. Pre-fix, all of those compiled to \"no-op surrounding text() runs, branches drop silently\" — extremely hard to debug because the closure FIRED, the surrounding statements WORKED, only the control-flow body disappeared.\n\n**How to apply:** Resolved by zyntax commit `ac32934e0ce4651048a0ea8b3733283d379d1577`. Blinc's matching commit is `e0a105af`. When writing DSL grammar that lowers to control-flow inside a closure body, always prefer `TypedExpression::If` (expression form) — even at top level. The expression-form Cranelift path is the one zyntax exercises in tests. See [[gotcha-zyntax-lambda-param-typed-ast-types]] for the related typed_ast var inheritance issue.","files":null}]}